        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Erlitou culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> valley: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, later also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>; the type site at Erlitou in Yanshi, Henan, on the Yi River, a tributary of the Luo.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Early estimates c. 1900–1500 BCE; radiocarbon dating since 2007 gives 1750–1500 BCE. The Erlitou site grew in four phases; Phase IV overlaps the Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erligang-culture">Erligang culture</a> (1600–1450 BCE), and in the Upper Erligang phase (1450–1300 BCE) Erlitou shrank to a village.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the palace city of Erlitou, with its walled palace complex and courtyards; the earliest large-scale bronze casting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in section moulds; the earliest bronze ding and the first bronze dagger-axes; early metal bells; jade ritual blades (zhang) and dagger-axes (ge); the earliest tracks of wheeled vehicles in China; symbols on pottery</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at Erlitou, discovered in 1959 by Xu Xusheng; radiocarbon dating since 2007; the Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project; traditional accounts written down much later, such as the Bamboo Annals and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>. No contemporary written records have been found.</span></div>
        </div>
