    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Most archaeologists consider the Erlitou culture, centred on a palace city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, the first state-level society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>Erlitou was the earliest large-scale bronze-producing culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the city monopolised the casting of ritual bronze vessels, among them the earliest ding.</li><li>Chinese archaeologists generally identify it with the later part of the Xia dynasty, but some Western scholars call this premature, since no contemporary writing has been found to prove the link.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Tracks of vehicles found near the palace area at Erlitou are the earliest evidence of wheeled wagons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

